BLOCKING

F1. packages/commands/src/run.test.ts:1367-1370 — W-121's test.sleep ratchet is routed around rather than satisfied.
Commit d62190c replaced a correctly annotated site
    // sleep-seam: heldAt -- two samples a short wait apart; a host still reading would have served more by the second
    await new Promise((ok) => setTimeout(ok, 500));
with
    const settleUntil = Date.now() + 500;
    await waitFor("the settle window to pass", () => Date.now() >= settleUntil, 5_000);
and the commit message states the reason: "The annotated-sleep census (rules/tests.test.ts, SEAM_CAP) is already at its cap of six, so a seventh seam in run.test.ts failed it."
The predicate is the clock and nothing else, so this is the same wall-clock sleep with the primitive removed. The scanner's grammar (packages/cli/src/rules/tests.ts:41-50) sees no primitive, so no site is registered, no annotation is required, and the ratchet at packages/cli/src/rules/tests.test.ts:652 (seams.length <= SEAM_CAP) never fires. Confirmed: check studio reports zero test.sleep findings at this tree.
A synchronisation seam exists and the row already uses it: sample() (run.test.ts:1267-1274) issues a host command and waitFor's on an observable, i.e. it round-trips the host's event loop. Two consecutive sample() calls prove the loop turned twice while host.served() did not grow — no clock needed. The 400 ms and 500 ms numbers carry no information the served count does not.
W-132 does not own packages/cli/src/rules/tests.test.ts, so raising the cap was correctly out of reach; the available routes were to drop the wall-clock wait (above) or to stop and escalate. Reshaping the construct until the detector misses it is neither.
This is the third appearance of class tests x wall-clock-sleep-for-a-missing-seam (L-035, L-050; check already reports it recurrent with nothing naming what addresses it) and it also instantiates process x insurance-clause-fired (L-020, L-041): a mechanism that fired was routed around after it fired.

