F2. packages/commands/src/run.test.ts:1355-1360 (commit c2cba4b) — the second instance of the same class: a 400 ms quiescence debounce, Date.now() - stableSince >= 400. It does ride a real observable (host.served().length), which makes it materially better than F1, but it is the same clock-shaped wait slipped past the same census. Expressing both through sample() round-trips removes both.

F3. The one-frame reply bound is about 256 MiB, not 4 MiB; the brief is wrong and the builder's report is right.
studio/briefs/W-132.md:134 and :142 claim the held frame is "at most the 4 MiB stdout cap (run-builder-host.mjs:298)". Line 298-299 caps only launch()'s capture; the broker's serve path goes through git() at scripts/run-builder-host.mjs:99 with maxBuffer: 256 * 1024 * 1024. scripts/git-broker.mjs:91-95 (reply()) builds Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(frame)) with no size check.
Measured (frame-bound.log): a 64 MiB reply to a deaf cell leaves ~126 MiB retained and 181 MiB RSS, held indefinitely while the request stream stays paused. Reachable from the allowlist (diff -p / log -p over cell-authored content).
Not blocking. The exposure is W-125's and is unchanged by this opus: the socket path queued the same reply in net.Socket's write buffer. Every obligation D-035 names still holds — policy stays host-owned (run-builder-host.mjs:528-546), no raw .git write, the host parses only the pipes it created, teardown closes and deletes. The frame is never queued, the host stays live, recovers and exits, and 4a tests all three. Recommend a follow-on that caps the reply frame in git-broker.mjs (or lowers git()'s maxBuffer) and corrects those two sentences of the brief.

F4. Deviation 1 (non-blocking readSync polled at 5 ms instead of a stream) is sound, and the brief is now stale. studio/briefs/W-132.md:127-128 prescribes git-request as "O_RDWR, read through a stream"; scripts/git-broker.mjs:65 and :126-166 use O_RDWR | O_NONBLOCK with polled readSync from the unref'd timer. The builder's reason holds: an fs ReadStream on a FIFO leaves an uncancellable threadpool read pending, which is exactly the non-exit hazard the brief itself names at lines 67-68. The code is right; the brief text should be amended at the next architect touch. Cost is bounded (<=5 ms per call, WORK=16 per tick).

F5. Deviation 4 (mkfifo shell-out) accepted. scripts/git-broker.mjs:57-60 spawns mkfifo -m 600 with env: { PATH: "/usr/bin:/bin" }. Node has no mkfifo binding; the environment is fully replaced (no LD_PRELOAD), shell is false, both paths are host-derived from runtime/, -m sets the mode exactly without umask, and no cell input reaches the call. It is a new undeclared host-binary dependency (coreutils), alongside the existing bwrap and git ones; worth a line in the brief's runtime assumptions.

F6. Deviation 5 (no new red for 4a) accepted, and the gap is covered by mutation. studio/ci/reds/W-132/04.log is the absent-module assertion and does not exercise 4a, so 4a's assertions have never run red; the architect ruled this at revision 2. Mitigated by two mutants against the final module, each killed by a 4a assertion specifically: replyFd opened blocking kills "b4a: the host answers a sample after the flood" (mutant1-b4-final.log), and dropping the request-pause kills "b4a: the host served fewer frames than the cell got in" (mutant2-b4-final.log). QA lex 2: one mutation per opus suffices.

